WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.138
"Anne, you are the most exasperating girl!

2
00:00:04.139 --> 00:00:08.686
What is it that you've done?" Marilla exclaimed.

3
00:00:08.687 --> 00:00:13.344
"I've sold Mr. Harrison's cow to Mr. Shearer!

4
00:00:13.345 --> 00:00:18.195
Dolly is out in the milking pen this very minute," Anne said.

5
00:00:18.196 --> 00:00:22.002
"Anne Shirley, are you dreaming?" asked Marilla.

6
00:00:22.003 --> 00:00:28.076
"I wish I were," said Anne. "But I feel like I'm having a nightmare.

7
00:00:28.077 --> 00:00:35.657
Mr. Harrison's cow is on the other side of the island by now. What can I do?"

8
00:00:35.658 --> 00:00:41.722
"Anne, you must tell Mr. Harrison right now," Marilla said sternly.

9
00:00:41.723 --> 00:00:46.533
"I'm sure he'll be awfully angry," said Anne.

10
00:00:46.534 --> 00:00:52.895
"He probably will," said Marilla. "I suppose I can talk to him."

11
00:00:52.896 --> 00:00:58.638
"No," exclaimed Anne. "It's my fault—I'll go."

12
00:00:58.639 --> 00:01:02.235
As Anne left she saw a cake she had baked that morning.

13
00:01:02.236 --> 00:01:06.380
She decided to take it with her as a peace offering.

14
00:01:06.381 --> 00:01:11.396
Mr. Harrison was sitting on his veranda, enjoying his evening pipe.

15
00:01:11.397 --> 00:01:16.844
When he saw Anne, he sprang to his feet, ran into the house, and shut the door.

16
00:01:16.845 --> 00:01:23.892
"If he was angry before, he'll be full of rage when he hears what I've done," Anne thought miserably.

17
00:01:23.893 --> 00:01:28.158
Anne knocked on the door, and Mr. Harrison opened it.

18
00:01:28.159 --> 00:01:34.609
Surprisingly, he spoke in a friendly, though nervous, tone and invited Anne inside.

19
00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:38.632
Mr. Harrison politely offered Anne a dusty chair.

20
00:01:38.633 --> 00:01:45.515
Everything was quite pleasant until Ginger the parrot exclaimed, "Who's that redheaded smarty-pants?"

21
00:01:45.516 --> 00:01:51.855
It was hard to say whose face went redder: Mr. Harrison's or Anne's.

22
00:01:51.856 --> 00:01:57.589
"Don't mind that parrot," said Mr. Harrison, casting a furious glance at Ginger.

23
00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:03.714
"He's . . . he's always talking nonsense. I got him from my brother who was a sailor."

24
00:02:03.715 --> 00:02:12.758
"That's all right," murmured Anne. "Mr. Harrison, I have something to confess. It's about the cow."

25
00:02:12.759 --> 00:02:19.534
"Was that cow in my oats again? Well, never mind," Mr. Harrison said.

26
00:02:19.535 --> 00:02:24.611
"I was too angry yesterday. Sometimes I have a bad temper.

27
00:02:24.612 --> 00:02:27.354
The oats will be all right, I'm sure."

28
00:02:27.355 --> 00:02:32.181
"Actually, it's ten times worse," Anne said.

29
00:02:32.182 --> 00:02:35.918
"Was the cow in my wheat?" asked Mr. Harrison.

30
00:02:35.919 --> 00:02:40.264
"No, not the wheat," Anne replied.

31
00:02:40.265 --> 00:02:45.642
"The cabbages ?" asked Mr. Harrison. "My prize

32
00:02:45.643 --> 00:02:54.553
cabbages?" "No, Mr. Harrison," said Anne anxiously. "I . . . I've sold your

33
00:02:54.554 --> 00:03:01.233
cow. It was an accident and I'm very, very sorry." Anne then explained what had

34
00:03:01.234 --> 00:03:08.744
happened. "Well," said Mr. Harrison slowly, "accidents happen, and I will take Dolly in

35
00:03:08.745 --> 00:03:16.468
exchange." Anne breathed a sigh of relief and smiled meekly. "Would you like some

36
00:03:16.469 --> 00:03:22.163
cake?" And so began the unlikely friendship of Mr. Harrison and

37
00:03:22.164 --> 00:03:28.108
Anne. A few days later, Anne, Gilbert Blythe, and Jane Andrews were walking

38
00:03:28.109 --> 00:03:32.447
together. The next day they would all start their new jobs as

39
00:03:32.448 --> 00:03:37.226
teachers. They were discussing how bad students should be

40
00:03:37.227 --> 00:03:41.689
punished. "I could never hit a child," Anne said

41
00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:47.614
passionately. "Miss Stacy never hit anyone, and she had perfect order when she taught us.

42
00:03:47.615 --> 00:03:53.177
Mr. Phillips hit students all the time, and he never had order!"

43
00:03:53.178 --> 00:03:55.841
"But what if the children don't obey?

44
00:03:55.842 --> 00:04:01.753
I think it's a good punishment," said Jane. "What do you think, Gilbert?"

45
00:04:01.754 --> 00:04:09.361
"Well," Gilbert said slowly. He agreed with Jane, but he wanted Anne's approval.

46
00:04:09.362 --> 00:04:14.232
"I don't believe in hitting children much—maybe just as a last resort."

47
00:04:14.233 --> 00:04:17.281
Anne and Jane both scowled at him.

48
00:04:17.282 --> 00:04:23.471
Gilbert had tried to please both and, as a result, he had pleased nobody.

49
00:04:23.472 --> 00:04:33.058
When Anne entered the classroom of the Avonlea School the next morning, she was confronted by rows of shining faces with bright, inquisitive eyes.

50
00:04:33.059 --> 00:04:37.910
Anne hoped she did not look as frightened and foolish as she felt.

51
00:04:37.911 --> 00:04:46.105
She had stayed up almost until midnight composing a speech for her students. She opened her mouth to begin.

52
00:04:46.106 --> 00:04:56.895
Even though it was a good speech with some very fine ideas, especially about cooperation and the joy of learning, Anne could not remember a word of it.

53
00:04:56.896 --> 00:05:08.764
After what seemed to her a year—about ten seconds in reality—Anne said faintly, "Take out your English books, please," and sat down.

54
00:05:08.765 --> 00:05:13.532
Anne looked at her new students as they read the first chapter.

55
00:05:13.533 --> 00:05:20.853
She knew most of them because, only a year ago, she had been a student herself and had gone to school with them.

56
00:05:20.854 --> 00:05:27.005
There were also ten new students. Sitting at a corner desk was Anthony Pye.

57
00:05:27.006 --> 00:05:33.443
He had a dark, sullen little face and was staring at Anne with a hostile expression.

58
00:05:33.444 --> 00:05:39.851
Three pale little girls, all crowded into one seat, were from the Cotton family.

59
00:05:39.852 --> 00:05:43.092
A handsome little boy sat in the front of the room.

60
00:05:43.093 --> 00:05:48.353
Anne thought he must be Paul Irving, whose father had once lived in Avonlea.

61
00:05:48.354 --> 00:05:53.678
When Paul's mother died, Paul had come to Avonlea to live with his grandmother.

62
00:05:53.679 --> 00:06:01.339
Anne and Paul smiled at each other, and Anne felt that each had recognized a kindred spirit in the other.

63
00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:07.176
Throughout the day the children behaved quite well; only two cases of discipline occurred.

64
00:06:07.177 --> 00:06:10.407
Morley Andrews was caught with a pair of crickets.

65
00:06:10.408 --> 00:06:16.463
Anne confiscated the crickets and made Morley stand on the lecture platform for an hour.

66
00:06:16.464 --> 00:06:20.559
She put the crickets in a box and later set them free.

67
00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:28.575
However, from that day on, Morley always believed Anne had taken them home and kept them as pets.

68
00:06:28.576 --> 00:06:35.312
The other culprit was Anthony Pye, who poured water down the back of the youngest Cotton girl's neck.

69
00:06:35.313 --> 00:06:44.552
Anne gave Anthony a lecture that was quite kind and touching, but Anthony remained unfazed and whistled scornfully when she finished.

70
00:06:44.553 --> 00:06:49.548
At three o'clock Anne rang the bell and watched her students go home.

71
00:06:49.549 --> 00:07:01.524
She resolved to love all of them, even Anthony Pye.

